# Hilbert Polynomial of the Twisted Cubic

## Setup

The twisted cubic $C \subset \mathbb{P}^3$ is the image of the Veronese embedding
$$\nu_3 : \mathbb{P}^1 \to \mathbb{P}^3, \qquad [s:t] \mapsto [s^3 : s^2 t : s t^2 : t^3] = [X:Y:Z:W].$$

Its homogeneous coordinate ring is
$$S(C) = k[s^3, s^2 t, s t^2, t^3] = \bigoplus_{n \ge 0} k[s,t]_{3n} \subset k[s,t],$$
the $3$rd Veronese subring of $k[s,t]$. The Hilbert function is
$$H_C(n) = \dim_k S(C)_n = \dim_k k[s,t]_{3n} = 3n + 1.$$

## Surjectivity of the multiplication map

Let $V = \operatorname{span}\{s^3, s^2 t, s t^2, t^3\}$, so $S(C)_n$ is the image of $\operatorname{Sym}^n V \to k[s,t]_{3n}$. This map is **surjective for every $n \ge 0$**: every monomial $s^{3n-i}t^i$ ($0 \le i \le 3n$) is a product of $n$ cubic monomials. Concretely, write $i = 3q + r$ with $r \in \{0,1,2\}$; then
$$s^{3n-i}t^i = (s^3)^{n-q-1}(s^{3-r}t^r)(t^3)^q \quad (\text{with the }(s^3)\text{ factor omitted when }q = n, \text{ etc.}),$$
using factors $s^3, s^2 t, s t^2, t^3$ as appropriate. Hence $H_C(n) = 3n+1$ for **all** $n \ge 0$, and the Hilbert polynomial — which agrees with the Hilbert function for all $n \ge 0$ here — is

$$\boxed{P_C(n) = 3n + 1.}$$

## Cross-check via the genus formula

For a smooth projective curve of degree $d$ and (arithmetic) genus $g$ in $\mathbb{P}^r$, the Hilbert polynomial is
$$P_C(n) = d\,n + 1 - g.$$

The twisted cubic has $d = 3$ and $g = 0$ (it is isomorphic to $\mathbb{P}^1$), giving $P_C(n) = 3n + 1 - 0 = 3n + 1$, confirming the direct computation.

## Verification of the ideal

The given ideal $(XZ - Y^2,\; YW - Z^2,\; XW - YZ)$ is exactly the ideal of $2 \times 2$ minors of the catalecticant matrix
$$\begin{pmatrix} X & Y & Z \\ Y & Z & W \end{pmatrix},$$
which is the classical defining ideal of the twisted cubic. So the curve in the problem is indeed the rational normal curve of degree $3$.

## Answer

$$P_C(n) = 3n + 1.$$

